// known-fail: on the shipping compiler (0.0.241) this exits 3 -- the A5 RAII
// auto-free is dead code. It exits 0 on the 0.0.242 WIP build (detector fixed).
// DO NOT add this to a manifest (EXPECTED.tsv OR EXPECTED_STDLIB.tsv).
// Enabling the auto-free on 0.0.242 breaks 26 EXPECTED rows, and the blocker is
// UNSOUND OWNERSHIP, not a detector that still needs work:
//   * lib/std/fmt.quanta fmt_print_char does `let buf = mem_alloc(3)` (owned ->
//     auto-freed at scope exit) and then `free(buf)` -- a DOUBLE FREE, because
//     `free` is not mem_free/drop and the RAII pass does not know that;
//   * functions that RETURN a mem_alloc buffer (formatted strings) hand the
//     caller a block the RAII pass also frees.
// Marking a block-scoped mem_alloc owned is correct only when the pointer cannot
// escape (returned, stored, captured, passed to a retainer), and deciding that
// needs escape/alias analysis, which does not exist. See
// compiler/0.0.242/WIP.md sections 5b-5c. The row becomes registrable when
// escape detection lands.
//
// Run manually:
//   compiler/<VER>/bin/x86/qc -O tests/raii_autofree_test.quanta /tmp/raii && \
//     ./scripts/quanta_run.sh /tmp/raii < /dev/null ; echo rc=$?
//   -> 0.0.241: rc=3 (blocks never freed)     0.0.242 WIP: rc=0
//
// raii_autofree_test.quanta - A5 (0.0.242): the P2 RAII auto-free for a
// block-scoped `mem_alloc` binding must actually run at scope exit.
//
// RED-FIRST: on the pre-fix compiler this row exits 3. The detector at
// parse.quanta was DEAD CODE (it read the IR_MOV record at irc-1 instead of the
// IR_CALL at irc-2, and read the arg COUNT / arg-start INDEX as the callee name
// offset / length), so `vreg_owned` was never set and IR_FREE was never
// emitted: the blocks below were never pushed onto the free list.
//
// The assertions pin the EXACT free-list behaviour, not just "no crash":
//   * a correct free leaves the block's length header [p] = n UNTOUCHED and
//     writes [p+8] = the OLD free-list head, then sets the head to p;
//   * two sequential frees therefore CHAIN: [p2+8] == p1 and [p1+8] == 0.
// That chain also proves HEAP_CTRL itself was written (p2's link value could
// only be p1 if p1's free stored it as the head).
//
// It also discriminates the two ways a fix can be wrong:
//   * no free at all            -> [p1+8] stays 111, [p2+8] stays 222  -> exit 3
//   * free with a mis-encoded store (the pre-fix IR_FREE handler wrote rbx
//     into [rbx] and [rbx+8], clobbering the length header)              -> exit 1
//   * correct free                                                       -> exit 0
//
// Relying on the block being readable after its scope closed is safe here for a
// stated reason: free-list REUSE is deferred (mem_alloc ignores the free list,
// emitter.quanta), so the mapping is never unmapped or handed out again; we are
// reading a still-live mapping, deterministically.
fn main() {
    let s1 = 0
    let s2 = 0
    {
        let p1 = mem_alloc(4)
        mem_store(p1 + 8, 111)
        s1 = p1                 // escape the pointer value (not the binding)
    }                           // scope exit: p1 must be auto-freed here
    {
        let p2 = mem_alloc(4)
        mem_store(p2 + 8, 222)
        s2 = p2
    }                           // scope exit: p2 must be auto-freed here

    // Header must survive: a correct free does NOT write [p].
    if mem_load(s1) != 4 { exit(1) }
    if mem_load(s2) != 4 { exit(2) }
    // The free-list chain: head=p2, [p2+8]=p1, [p1+8]=0.
    if mem_load(s2 + 8) != s1 { exit(3) }
    if mem_load(s1 + 8) != 0 { exit(4) }
    exit(0)
}
